.TH PROC_FIND 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
proc_find \- find a process by PID
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <pm/pm.h>
.LP
.BI "process_t *proc_find(int " pid ");
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B proc_find
function searches the kernel's process table for a process with the
specified process identifier
.IR pid .
.PP
This function provides a safe, centralized way to look up processes
by PID, replacing the previous pattern of iterating through the
.B processes[]
array directly.
.SH RETURN VALUES
On success,
.B proc_find
returns a pointer to the
.I process_t
structure for the requested process.
.PP
If no process with the given PID exists (or if the slot is unused),
.B proc_find
returns NULL.
.SH NOTES
.IP \[bu] 2
The returned pointer is valid only as long as the process exists.
For safe access across context switches or when the process might
exit, callers should hold
.B proctree_lock
or disable interrupts.
.IP \[bu]
The function does not acquire any locks; the caller is responsible
for locking if required.
.IP \[bu]
A process slot with
.B pid == -1
is considered free/unused.
.IP \[bu]
The maximum number of processes is defined by
.B MAX_PROCS
in
.IR <pm/pm.h> .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <pm/pm.h>

/* Find and print process name */
process_t *p = proc_find(target_pid);
if (p) {
    kprint("Process %d: %s\\n", p->pid, p->comm);
} else {
    kprint("Process %d not found\\n", target_pid);
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proc_create (9),
.BR proc_fork (9),
.BR pm_init (9)
